{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace","id":987764,"title":"Impressions: Social Maneuvering, Vampires and Embrace","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-10-15T08:28:28-07:00","id":987764,"title":"Impressions: Social Maneuvering, Vampires and Embrace","content":"Hy, Stortytellers =)<br /> <br /> About social maneuvers, I would like to know your impressions.<br /> You guys use this system at your vampire table? How's that for you?<br /> The mechanics says that &quot;If the announced goal would be a breaking point for the victim, add two Doors.&quot; But if the goal is to convince a vampire to embrace his proteg\u00e9 - something that costs one humanity dot to the target - how many doors should I add? How do you guys deal whit this kind of situation?<br /> <br /> Thanks for the oportunity to post here,<br /> and good chronicles for all!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11934-draugr","name":"Draugr","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11934_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-15T08:41:33-07:00","id":987772,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Draugr</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace#post987764\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">About social maneuvers, I would like to know your impressions.</div> </div> </div> </div> Its at a Good Impression, so that's one roll a day for me!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You guys use this system at your vampire table? How's that for you? </div> </div> </div> Yep, both PCs to NPCs, and other way around. No PC to PC. Works great, and the players generally like the Conditions I impose. Sometimes they're funny, sometimes they're interesting, sometimes they just want the beat and are willing to do it for that. Othertimes, they just ignore it and keep going. Incentives all around, no sticks.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The mechanics says that &quot;If the announced goal would be a breaking point for the victim, add two Doors.&quot; But if the goal is to convince a vampire to embrace his proteg\u00e9 - something that costs one humanity dot to the target - how many doors should I add? How do you guys deal whit this kind of situation? </div> </div> </div> Honestly, I'd assign a number of Doors based on the personal rejection of the target. While it may cost Humanity, making a childer might be something that intrests the target, and adding Doors is inappropriate. The target might be horrified by the prospect, and I'd add more than two. <br /> <br /> Breaking points tend to be something that a target doesn't want to happen in a lot of cases, and causes some spiritual shock. Making childer could be something desirable, could be undesirable, so I don't think that breaking points are something of a good comparison right here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-15T09:02:54-07:00","id":987779,"title":"","content":"I absolutely love the Social Maneuvering system. I use it for all Social actions and it works well for me as a means of tracking progress in the process of manipulating NPCs instead of simple Skill checks. I'm pretty generous with first Impressions, Good is easy to get to, simply by being personable. Excellent requires some kind of effort, usually constituting a Skill check of some sort. Perfect is only possible with Soft Leverage, but I'm also pretty liberal with what counts as Soft Leverage - a simple &quot;Let me buy you lunch&quot; will often suffice. As a result, players generally begin Social actions by trying to endear themselves to their mark, and most Social Maneuvering is made at Excellent or Perfect Impressions, unless they blunder and offend the mark, or the mark is suspicious of the PC. If the players can't get what they want from their marks in a reasonable amount of time, they'll open as many Doors as they can and finish off with Forcing Doors, unless the mark is a really important NPC that they don't want to risk burning bridges with. I also don't make the damage a failed Forcing Doors attempt completely irreparable.<br /> <br /> I agree with MCN about adding Doors for Embrace. Breaking Points usually add Doors because they're undesirable for the mark, but Embrace is automatic Humanity loss for other reasons, and some characters might or might not have any problem with it. So it depends on how the vampire feels about Embracing their prodigy. If it's something they were already considering, I might even remove Doors.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 10-15-2016, 12:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-21T21:34:19-07:00","id":991270,"title":"","content":"Hy Storytellers!<br /> I\u2019m very grateful for the answers. Thanks.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=987772#post987772\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Its at a Good Impression, so that's one roll a day for me!<br /> <br /> Yep, both PCs to NPCs, and other way around. No PC to PC. Works great, and the players generally like the Conditions I impose. Sometimes they're funny, sometimes they're interesting, sometimes they just want the beat and are willing to do it for that. Othertimes, they just ignore it and keep going. Incentives all around, no sticks.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>For some reason, I don\u2019t feel good about using Social Maneuvers on PCs. But I've never tried it before, so you made me wonder about the feedback my players could give me..<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=987779#post987779\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I absolutely love the Social Maneuvering system. I use it for all Social actions and it works well for me as a means of tracking progress in the process of manipulating NPCs instead of simple Skill checks. I'm pretty generous with first Impressions, Good is easy to get to, simply by being personable. Excellent requires some kind of effort, usually constituting a Skill check of some sort. Perfect is only possible with Soft Leverage, but I'm also pretty liberal with what counts as Soft Leverage - a simple &quot;Let me buy you lunch&quot; will often suffice. As a result, players generally begin Social actions by trying to endear themselves to their mark, and most Social Maneuvering is made at Excellent or Perfect Impressions, unless they blunder and offend the mark, or the mark is suspicious of the PC. If the players can't get what they want from their marks in a reasonable amount of time, they'll open as many Doors as they can and finish off with Forcing Doors, unless the mark is a really important NPC that they don't want to risk burning bridges with. I also don't make the damage a failed Forcing Doors attempt completely irreparable.</div> </div> </div> </div>I feel the same about the system, however I\u2019m a bit less generous with the impressions. But I agree with the effect of Soft Leverage\u2026 given the context, &quot;Let me buy you lunch&quot; is a wonderful leverage, since the PC IS the lunch in this case XD<br /> <br /> Interesting the way your PCs react. My group have never experienced a Forcing Door attempt until now, and I can\u2019t imagine why. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=987772#post987772\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly, I'd assign a number of Doors based on the personal rejection of the target. While it may cost Humanity, making a childer might be something that intrests the target, and adding Doors is inappropriate. The target might be horrified by the prospect, and I'd add more than two.<br /> <br /> Breaking points tend to be something that a target doesn't want to happen in a lot of cases, and causes some spiritual shock. Making childer could be something desirable, could be undesirable, so I don't think that breaking points are something of a good comparison right here.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=987779#post987779\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> I agree with MCN about adding Doors for Embrace. Breaking Points usually add Doors because they're undesirable for the mark, but Embrace is automatic Humanity loss for other reasons, and some characters might or might not have any problem with it. So it depends on how the vampire feels about Embracing their prodigy. If it's something they were already considering, I might even remove Doors.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree with both of you. In this especific case, the vampire is passionate about the &quot;warm humanity&quot;, the &quot;taste of life&quot; of his blood doll (PC), and just something irreversible and tragic could convince him to Embrace her. Maybe it works like some kind of aspiration at an oposite way, plus an act against his very sense of right.. so.. +3 doors, I think.<br /> <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11934-draugr","name":"Draugr","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11934_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-21T22:25:31-07:00","id":991282,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Draugr</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=991270#post991270\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For some reason, I don\u2019t feel good about using Social Maneuvers on PCs. But I've never tried it before, so you made me wonder about the feedback my players could give me..</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm a recovered LARPer, so I'm pretty used to Social actions against PCs <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> What I do though, is I just have the PC gain the Leveraged Condition when all their Doors are opened. That way the player is never forced into an action they don't want their character to take, they retain agency, but they can get a Beat for doing something the NPC (or other PC) wants from them, either immediately or at a later convenience.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Draugr</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=991270#post991270\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I feel the same about the system, however I\u2019m a bit less generous with the impressions. But I agree with the effect of Soft Leverage\u2026 given the context, &quot;Let me buy you lunch&quot; is a wonderful leverage, since the PC IS the lunch in this case XD</div> </div> </div> </div>My generosity with Impressions is actually strictly by-the-book. Check out Chronicles of Darkness pages 81 and 82.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> \u201cAverage impressions\u201d call for weekly rolls, which makes the process very slow. Through play, your character may influence the interaction for a \u201cgood impression.\u201d This may mean meeting in a pleasant environment, wearing appealing clothing, playing appropriate music, or otherwise making the situation more comfortable. This should not require a roll during a first impression, but requires one if attempted later. An excellent impression requires a roll to influence the situation. For example, you may use a Wits + Socialize to find the right people to invite to a party. Perfect impressions require further factors. It may involve leverage, or playing to a character\u2019s Vice. </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Draugr</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=991270#post991270\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Interesting the way your PCs react. My group have never experienced a Forcing Door attempt until now, and I can\u2019t imagine why.</div> </div> </div> </div>Probably mostly due to the fact that the way it's written makes it sound like Forcing Doors necessarily means threatening the mark and should be an absolute last resort. But in my interpretation (which I believe to be the intent of the rule) is that Forcing Doors is simply an attempt to pressure the mark into making an immediate decision. When people talk about using single-roll Social actions instead of Social Maneuvering I just have to scratch my head because single-roll Social actions is exactly what Forcing Doors is, and it's <i>part of</i> the Social Maneuvering system.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-22T04:47:21-07:00","id":991340,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Draugr</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=991270#post991270\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hy Storytellers!<br /> <br /> For some reason, I don\u2019t feel good about using Social Maneuvers on PCs. But I've never tried it before, so you made me wonder about the feedback my players could give me.</div> </div> </div> </div> A lot of people think of Social Maneuvering as &quot;social combat.&quot; Its not. This isn't mind control. This is a method of providing plot hooks and drama as well as beats. I actually encourage some bit of PC - PC social maneuvering simply because it provides a way of sharing beats for helping each other with their Aspirations. Mechanical incentives to work together to make story. I personally find being the target of a social maneuvering attempt to be -exciting- than an issue - plot plus XP equals fun.<br /> <br /> What you do, for PCs, is focus on using Social Maneuvering to grant Conditions and beats. You give them mechanical incentives to go along with things. I particularly like using Inspired - the PCs not only get beats for doing whatever is requested, but have an easier time doing it as well. As well, keep in mind that all characters, PC or NPC, can just say no if there's no chance in hell.<br /> <br /> Admittedly, there's a lot of bad history to overcome with this kind of thing, and it still might not be the kind of game a lot of people want. But I find that its really fun and helpful.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-24T15:39:23-07:00","id":992593,"title":"","content":"<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=991282#post991282\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm a recovered LARPer, so I'm pretty used to Social actions against PCs <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> What I do though, is I just have the PC gain the Leveraged Condition when all their Doors are opened. That way the player is never forced into an action they don't want their character to take, they retain agency, but they can get a Beat for doing something the NPC (or other PC) wants from them, either immediately or at a later convenience.<br /> <br /> Probably mostly due to the fact that the way it's written makes it sound like Forcing Doors necessarily means threatening the mark and should be an absolute last resort. But in my interpretation (which I believe to be the intent of the rule) is that Forcing Doors is simply an attempt to pressure the mark into making an immediate decision. When people talk about using single-roll Social actions instead of Social Maneuvering I just have to scratch my head because single-roll Social actions is exactly what Forcing Doors is, and it's <i>part of</i> the Social Maneuvering system.</div> </div> </div> </div>Definitely, I'll try the PC / PC social Maneuver next time the chance appear. Let's see how it works for us...<br /> About Forcing doors, I agree 100%. Now that you mention it, I realize that they do force doors, but only in ordinary issues. When they really want something, plan on it, aspire to it, we use to do much building relationship roleplay, forgeting the dice roll...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=991340#post991340\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> A lot of people think of Social Maneuvering as &quot;social combat.&quot; Its not. This isn't mind control. This is a method of providing plot hooks and drama as well as beats. I actually encourage some bit of PC - PC social maneuvering simply because it provides a way of sharing beats for helping each other with their Aspirations. Mechanical incentives to work together to make story. I personally find being the target of a social maneuvering attempt to be -exciting- than an issue - plot plus XP equals fun.<br /> What you do, for PCs, is focus on using Social Maneuvering to grant Conditions and beats. You give them mechanical incentives to go along with things. I particularly like using Inspired - the PCs not only get beats for doing whatever is requested, but have an easier time doing it as well. As well, keep in mind that all characters, PC or NPC, can just say no if there's no chance in hell.<br /> Admittedly, there's a lot of bad history to overcome with this kind of thing, and it still might not be the kind of game a lot of people want. But I find that its really fun and helpful.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're right. For some people, all in the RPG is about &quot;some kind of combat&quot; ... I personally think the social mechanics maneuvers married to the concept of aspirations. The face of the challenge that the player wants to see achieved.<br /> <br /> thanks a lot, again, Charlaquin, MCN, for the aid and the cooperation to my first post. =)<br /> <br /> I'm very grateful!<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11934-draugr","name":"Draugr","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11934_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-25T08:38:14-07:00","id":992962,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=991282#post991282\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm a recovered LARPer, so I'm pretty used to Social actions against PCs <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> What I do though, is I just have the PC gain the Leveraged Condition when all their Doors are opened. That way the player is never forced into an action they don't want their character to take, they retain agency, but they can get a Beat for doing something the NPC (or other PC) wants from them, either immediately or at a later convenience.</div> </div> </div> </div>Similar here, the Leveraged Condition is a handy way to get the player to &quot;opt in&quot; for the beat, the masochism-carrot approach is an interesting way to get players to accept or even relish character setbacks. <a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/MiseryBuildsCharacter\" target=\"_blank\">It reminds me of </a><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenshiro\" target=\"_blank\">Kenshiro, from </a><a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/MiseryBuildsCharacter\" target=\"_blank\">Fist of The Northstar being thrown down a cliff </a>and <a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/StrongAsTheyNeedToBe\" target=\"_blank\">becoming incredibly powerful</a>. The more a player is willing to let their character be set back, the more they objectively grow in power. It is my thought that is if violent conflict is the only interaction that leaves lasting affects on player characters (especially since being affected by things is how you earn experience in this system, thus it makes combat the only route to experience, which is an extreme anachronistic style of role play) or NPCs then it'd be hard to blame the players for seeing it as the only truly valid tool for really interacting with the story. Social actions need consequences just like physical ones...<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">and in all likelihood players themselves are not a Sherlock Holmes or a Casanova any more than they are a Bruce Lee but their characters may very well be. If players characters only benefit from mechanics in combat because of some strange mental/social vs physical chauvinism that the player should somehow &quot;role play&quot; facilities they lack, when that is as unreasonable as having the player demonstrate bare knuckle boxing championship skills (boffer LARPs being more reasonable because at least there isn't as steep a segregation between physical/mental/social aspects of the game, at the other end of the spectrum). </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <span style=\"color:#008000\">^Spoiler includes feelings based on being a recovering LARPer as well, which could be construed as a &quot;rant&quot;.^</span><br /> <br /> -Relating to the Original Post-<br /> Asking what I would do, it is this: I'd added two Doors as per requiring a breaking point roll, and then check to see if there were factors that would ease the burden of getting through all the doors like the level of the impression, aligned Dirge or Aspiration and so on. It is important to factor as much in as you can think of to make the mechanics reflect in the in game experience so the mechanics then can be a proper framework for determining an outcome that makes sense given the factors. People when posting tend to focus on just one mechanic or modifier and not how the mechanics weave together to make a lattice which is integrated into the tapestry of the story as the threads of description cover it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":3279,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","user":"Pale_Crusader","date":". 10-26-2016, 10:16 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-31T08:32:11-07:00","id":996085,"title":"","content":"one problem I have with the social maneuvering system is the length of the interval depends on the impression and isnt affected at all by how small or great a thing is being asked.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/a/img829/1897/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/2iw20supportergarou125p\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imageshack.com/i/0j5hz8j\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imageshack.com/i/idnSdTJkj\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://i.imgsafe.org/97fd805379.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1153-prince-of-the-night","name":"Prince of the Night","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4205"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-31T09:05:05-07:00","id":996106,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prince of the Night</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=996085#post996085\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">one problem I have with the social maneuvering system is the length of the interval depends on the impression and isnt affected at all by how small or great a thing is being asked.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's what Doors are. How easy it is to convince them. Impression is how willing to let you try they are.<br /> <br /> The two extreme ends are covered by making it auto-success for trivial asks, and auto-fail for things they would never consider.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>(he/him/his)<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.kickstarter.com/projects/200664283/demon-the-descent-prestige-edition\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> Backer #2010","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1329-elvesofzion","name":"ElvesofZion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1329_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-31T14:33:29-07:00","id":996267,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prince of the Night</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=996085#post996085\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">one problem I have with the social maneuvering system is the length of the interval depends on the impression and isnt affected at all by how small or great a thing is being asked.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure it is. Asking for greater things adds extra Doors, which means you have to go through more iterations of the Impression-based duration.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-01T07:10:46-07:00","id":996567,"title":"","content":"I'll go right ahead and say I do not like the social maneuvering system. It's too complex for my liking and relies too much on dices. In my games, players actually don't throw a lot of dices. For social interactions, roleplaying is the most important and there are three cases. When it's logical for the NPC to go with what the PC wants (because of a nice roleplay, and good arguments) then no dice, the NPC goes with it. Same in reverse, if it's logical for the NPC to refuse because the arguments presented just cannot convince the NPC, then no dice and it fails. I only have them roll dices when their maneuvre (roleplayed) has a chance to fail and a chance to succeed. Then, it's usually a single dice roll to determine the outcome. I actually don't use extended tests too much since it slows down the game.<br /> <br /> The only time I may use some sort of a system is when the social maneuvre is secondary enough that it can happen off screen. But even then, I just determine how many phases there are, and players have to roll at the appropriate times. I don't bother with doors and what I call &quot;social health points&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12610-lestat","name":"Lestat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12610_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-01T08:42:00-07:00","id":996623,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lestat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=996567#post996567\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'll go right ahead and say I do not like the social maneuvering system. It's too complex for my liking and relies too much on dices. In my games, players actually don't throw a lot of dices. For social interactions, roleplaying is the most important and there are three cases. When it's logical for the NPC to go with what the PC wants (because of a nice roleplay, and good arguments) then no dice, the NPC goes with it. Same in reverse, if it's logical for the NPC to refuse because the arguments presented just cannot convince the NPC, then no dice and it fails. I only have them roll dices when their maneuvre (roleplayed) has a chance to fail and a chance to succeed. Then, it's usually a single dice roll to determine the outcome. I actually don't use extended tests too much since it slows down the game.<br /> <br /> The only time I may use some sort of a system is when the social maneuvre is secondary enough that it can happen off screen. But even then, I just determine how many phases there are, and players have to roll at the appropriate times. I don't bother with doors and what I call &quot;social health points&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The problem I have with this viewpoint was encapsulated perfectly by a friend of mine at my first LARP. &quot;Why waste points on social abilities. <b> I </b>actually have those&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5118-perverseness","name":"Perverseness","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"57"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-01T11:06:51-07:00","id":996696,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lestat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=996567#post996567\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'll go right ahead and say I do not like the social maneuvering system. It's too complex for my liking and relies too much on dices. In my games, players actually don't throw a lot of dices. For social interactions, roleplaying is the most important and there are three cases. When it's logical for the NPC to go with what the PC wants (because of a nice roleplay, and good arguments) then no dice, the NPC goes with it. Same in reverse, if it's logical for the NPC to refuse because the arguments presented just cannot convince the NPC, then no dice and it fails. I only have them roll dices when their maneuvre (roleplayed) has a chance to fail and a chance to succeed. Then, it's usually a single dice roll to determine the outcome. I actually don't use extended tests too much since it slows down the game.<br /> <br /> The only time I may use some sort of a system is when the social maneuvre is secondary enough that it can happen off screen. But even then, I just determine how many phases there are, and players have to roll at the appropriate times. I don't bother with doors and what I call &quot;social health points&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay so do you have characters who are more socially adept than your players? That's why you need social stats, so players can roll them and see how well they do. If you're basing how well they do strictly on how well the roleplay you're basically punishing them for putting points in those options.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-01T11:29:14-07:00","id":996709,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lestat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=996567#post996567\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'll go right ahead and say I do not like the social maneuvering system. It's too complex for my liking and relies too much on dices. In my games, players actually don't throw a lot of dices.</div> </div> </div> </div>They don't roll a lot of dice in my games either. But I love the Social Maneuvering system. It's really not as complicated as it looks.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lestat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=996567#post996567\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For social interactions, roleplaying is the most important and there are three cases. When it's logical for the NPC to go with what the PC wants (because of a nice roleplay, and good arguments) then no dice, the NPC goes with it. Same in reverse, if it's logical for the NPC to refuse because the arguments presented just cannot convince the NPC, then no dice and it fails. I only have them roll dices when their maneuvre (roleplayed) has a chance to fail and a chance to succeed.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is exactly how I do it too. Not just for Social actions, but for everything. This is adjudication 101, a fundamental concept of GMing. Dice are for resolving actions when the outcome is uncertain. If the outcome isn't uncertain, you don't need dice. Social Maneuvering can and should be used <i>within</i> this framework.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lestat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=996567#post996567\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Then, it's usually a single dice roll to determine the outcome. I actually don't use extended tests too much since it slows down the game.</div> </div> </div> </div>I do this too. This is adjudication 102. The only time you use multiple dice rolls to resolve the same action is when each roll increases the dramatic tension. Which it does when a character is trying to manipulate someone subtly. When they are taking a more direct approach, yes, you keep it to one single roll. That's called Forcing Doors, and it's part of the Social Maneuvering system.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-01T11:32:58-07:00","id":996710,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Resplendent Fire</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=996696#post996696\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Okay so do you have characters who are more socially adept than your players? That's why you need social stats, so players can roll them and see how well they do. If you're basing how well they do strictly on how well the roleplay you're basically punishing them for putting points in those options.</div> </div> </div> </div>Personally, I disagree with this. It's less that how well you do in social interactions is determined by how &quot;well&quot; you roleplay and more that any time dice are rolled they introduce a chance of success and a chance of failure, therefore dice shouldn't be rolled if one of those things doesn't exist. Also if there's no consequence for failure, nothing preventing the character from just trying the thing over and over again until they succeed, you can save yourself time and just say &quot;eventually, through perserverence, you are able to do the thing.&quot; That goes for every action, not just social ones.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 11-01-2016, 02:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-01T11:39:16-07:00","id":996712,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=996710#post996710\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally, I disagree with this. It's less that how well you do in social interactions is determined by how &quot;well&quot; you roleplay and more that any time dice are rolled they introduce a chance of success and a chance of failure, therefore dice shouldn't be rolled if one of those things doesn't exist. Also if there's no consequence for failure, nothing preventing the character from just trying the thing over and over again until they succeed, you can save yourself time and just say &quot;eventually, through perserverence, you are able to do the thing.&quot; That goes for every action, not just social ones.</div> </div> </div> </div>Right, but I wasn't talking about chance of success or failure, but rather using roleplay as the barometer of whether something succeeds. If I'm playing a social monster, she's going to be better at social stuff than I am, and if I am failing to achieve my goals socially because I'm expected to roleplay and never roll and can't muster the same degree of social competence as my character, there's a problem.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-01T11:49:11-07:00","id":996718,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=996710#post996710\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally, I disagree with this. It's less that how well you do in social interactions is determined by how &quot;well&quot; you roleplay and more that any time dice are rolled they introduce a chance of success and a chance of failure, therefore dice shouldn't be rolled if one of those things doesn't exist. Also if there's no consequence for failure, nothing preventing the character from just trying the thing over and over again until they succeed, you can save yourself time and just say &quot;eventually, through perserverence, you are able to do the thing.&quot; That goes for every action, not just social ones.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> \u200bI think that the part that <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2367_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2367\" data-vbnamecard=\"2367\">Resplendent Fire</a> was really disagree with was this '<i> relies too much on dices. In my games, players actually don't throw a lot of dices. For social interactions, roleplaying is the most important'</i> , it comes across as the old idea that 'role-playing' is purely talking with no reference to the character sheet, let alone die rolling. An idea that I also completely disagree with, rolling dice is <i>part</i> of role-playing, just as much as talking is.<br /> <br /> There is this bit ' <i>That's why you need social stats, so players can roll them'</i> that I disagree with Resplendent on, in that stats also inform how likely an action is to succeed or fail and thus whether you ever need to get to dice in the first place though. But that may not be a strong position for them, just a part of the way it was written.<br /> <br /> Edit: Partially Ninja'd<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Resplendent Fire</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=996712#post996712\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Right, but I wasn't talking about chance of success or failure, but rather using roleplay as the barometer of whether something succeeds. If I'm playing a social monster, she's going to be better at social stuff than I am, and if I am failing to achieve my goals socially because I'm expected to roleplay and never roll and can't muster the same degree of social competence as my character, there's a problem.</div> </div> </div> </div>I still think the need for 'rolling' is being overstated here. It could also just be that your character is good at socializing so no roleplaying/rolling is needed to determine success or failure. Which I believe is <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar269_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"269\" data-vbnamecard=\"269\">Charlaquin</a> 's point.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>(he/him/his)<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.kickstarter.com/projects/200664283/demon-the-descent-prestige-edition\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> Backer #2010","edited":{"userid":1329,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1329-elvesofzion","user":"ElvesofZion","date":". 11-01-2016, 02:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1329-elvesofzion","name":"ElvesofZion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1329_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-01T11:51:54-07:00","id":996721,"title":"","content":"You are missing my point. Or maybe I didn't clearly expressed it. I will try and develop my viewpoint. Well, at least, there are two sides to my answer:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Okay so do you have characters who are more socially adept than your players? That's why you need social stats, so players can roll them and see how well they do. If you're basing how well they do strictly on how well the roleplay you're basically punishing them for putting points in those options. </div> </div> </div>1) I've heard the argument about the difference between the player's social skills vs the character's about a dozen times before. The argument works well on paper, but in practice, a less socially inclined player playing a social character will have most of his interactions determined by dice rolls. It's boring. Now, when I assess what I call the &quot;player's roleplay&quot;, I do not speak about his charisma or the way he delivers his lines (or text, since we also play by forum). I speak more about the content of what he is saying. So now you could oppose me the argument that not all players have an ability to provide powerful arguments. You are right, but it goes back to my point where, if we only stick to what the character is supposed to know, roleplay becomes irrelevant. It would be very frustrating to my players if their social interactions were just reduced to a dice pool. They actually love that what they are saying is actually more important than dice pools. Even when it comes to combat, a well planned fight will be rewarded and combat dice pools positively impacted. Hell, even when characters are trying to unravel the scenario's mystery, you could tell me not all players are all gifted for that kind of stuff, and therefore they could just roll Intelligence+Investigation to determine if they unravel the plot. But it's no fun. All that being said, I never said I did not use dice pools, and it's not the only reason why I don't like the social maneuvering system. This leads me to my second point.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The problem I have with this viewpoint was encapsulated perfectly by a friend of mine at my first LARP. &quot;Why waste points on social abilities. <b>I </b>actually have those&quot; </div> </div> </div>2) I absolutely do use rolls. Just not as much as some. I agree that sometimes, the character's skills are more important than the player's. It happens in the case I mentionned in my previous post, when arguments are valid and can lead to a successful social maneuver, but not by themselves. This is when the character's skills can make the difference. This is when dices are rolled. When that happens, I have the player roll once. I don't need a serie of endless rolls. It's pointless to me. If I think the NPC cannot be convinced in a single interaction, I just determine how many interactions it will take, and I tend to integrate these into the plot, but it doesn't get more complex than that. The social maneuvering system is a tad too heavy. But then again, I completely houseruled the main rules about tests while still using the same character sheets so I guess it makes sense I set aside rules that are, to me, unnecessarily heavy.<br /> <br /> In conclusion, I believe there has to be a balance, in social interactions but also other areas, between the players' actual roleplay and ideas and their characters' skills. And to me, it should always be more inclined towards roleplay so it doesn't become a soulless dice fest.<br /> <br /> <br /> EDIT: damn, I missed a lot of answers ! Sorry, people. Charlaquin, I seem to connect with your style very well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12610,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12610-lestat","user":"Lestat","date":". 11-01-2016, 02:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12610-lestat","name":"Lestat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12610_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-01T12:01:30-07:00","id":996730,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lestat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=996721#post996721\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If I think the NPC cannot be convinced in a single interaction, I just determine how many interactions it will take, and I tend to integrate these into the plot, but it doesn't get more complex than that. The social maneuvering system is a tad too heavy.</div> </div> </div> </div>I didn't say this before, but you are basically using the Social Manevuring system if you do this. Just by eyeballing it instead of using the system to calculate the numbers. <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar269_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"269\" data-vbnamecard=\"269\">Charlaquin</a> has a very good write up about the system somewhere (don'[t remember where) and how she uses it that illustrates this well. And its fine to eyeball it, the system is really just a more codified way of doing what many more experienced GMs do already.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>(he/him/his)<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.kickstarter.com/projects/200664283/demon-the-descent-prestige-edition\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> Backer #2010","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1329-elvesofzion","name":"ElvesofZion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1329_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-01T12:04:26-07:00","id":996731,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Resplendent Fire</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=996712#post996712\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Right, but I wasn't talking about chance of success or failure, but rather using roleplay as the barometer of whether something succeeds. If I'm playing a social monster, she's going to be better at social stuff than I am, and if I am failing to achieve my goals socially because I'm expected to roleplay and never roll and can't muster the same degree of social competence as my character, there's a problem.</div> </div> </div> </div>Right, right. This is why I don't much care for the term &quot;how well you roleplay&quot; and put it in quotation marks. How does one roleplay &quot;well&quot; or &quot;poorly&quot;, and how does one use the quality of roleplay to judge the success of a social action? Roleplaying, to me, is simply imagining yourself as a different person or in a different situation, and making decisions based on how you think you or that person would act in that scenario. It's not really something you do to varying degrees of quality, you just do it or you don't. I think what people are getting at when they say &quot;roleplaying well&quot; is <i>acting</i> well, since acting is a skill, and one often used when roleplaying. Behaving in a convincing facsimile of how the person in the situation you are imagining might behave. And in that case, I agree with you that the quality of one's acting shouldn't be a determining factor in whether a social action (or any other action) succeeds or fails. What should matter is the roleplaying - the choices you make based on what you think the character would do in the imagined situation. Your actions, not your phrasing. That's why part of the GM's job, especially in social interactions, is to figure out the player's intended methods and approach, cause that's the real determining factor in whether or not dice should be rolled, not their wording or inflection.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":269,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","user":"Charlaquin","date":". 11-01-2016, 03:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-01T12:11:54-07:00","id":996735,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I didn't say this before, but you are basically using the Social Manevuring system if you do this. Just by eyeballing it instead of using the system to calculate the numbers. <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar269_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"269\" data-vbnamecard=\"269\">Charlaquin</a> has a very good write up about the system somewhere (don'[t remember where) and how she uses it that illustrates this well. And its fine to eyeball it, the system is really just a more codified way of doing what many more experienced GMs do already. </div> </div> </div>As I was reading through your posts, I started realizing what you are stating here. You might be right and the fact it's, as you are saying it well, more codified, is probably the aspect that bugs me the most. But I just reread the paragraph on social maneuvering, and yes, I will admit I might have read through it because it sounded more complex than what I usually prefer. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Your actions, not your phrasing. That's why part of the GM's job, especially in social interactions, is to figure out the player's intended methods and approach, cause that's the real determining factor in whether or not dice should be rolled, not their phrasing. </div> </div> </div>My point exactly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12610-lestat","name":"Lestat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12610_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"32"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-01T12:18:22-07:00","id":996740,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ElvesofZion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=996730#post996730\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">. <span class=\"b-bbcode-user js-bbcode-user\">Guest</span> has a very good write up about the system somewhere (don'[t remember where) and how she uses it that illustrates this well.</div> </div> </div> </div>If it's the one I'm thinking of, it was actually <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2758\" data-vbnamecard=\"2758\">Errol216</a>, not me. <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/913221-using-social-manuevering\">Here it is</a>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-01T12:20:53-07:00","id":996745,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lestat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=996735#post996735\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As I was reading through your posts, I started realizing what you are stating here. You might be right and the fact it's, as you are saying it well, more codified, is probably the aspect that bugs me the most. But I just reread the paragraph on social maneuvering, and yes, I will admit I might have read through it because it sounded more complex than what I usually prefer.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, Social Maneuvering has a bad case of looking more complex on paper than it actually is in play.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-01T12:27:15-07:00","id":996750,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/987764-impressions-social-maneuvering-vampires-and-embrace?p=996740#post996740\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If it's the one I'm thinking of, it was actually <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2758-errol216\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2758_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2758\" data-vbnamecard=\"2758\">Errol216</a>, not me. <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/913221-using-social-manuevering\">Here it is</a>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh maybe, maybe I mixed that up with your general passion for helping people to coming to terms with the system spread all over <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> Edit: Adding more responses so I don't double post.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12610-lestat\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar12610_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"12610\" data-vbnamecard=\"12610\">Lestat</a>, I really suggest reading the post linked above. It really helps to showcase how the system can be used without it feeling like its too complex. The long and short of it is: Social Maneuvering is <b>NOT</b> social combat, it is a longer form system for helping to determine how a character would react to being convinced of something. I just used it to explain the idea to my players in an upcoming mage game that looked at it and thought it was too complex too.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>(he/him/his)<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://www.kickstarter.com/projects/200664283/demon-the-descent-prestige-edition\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> Backer #2010","edited":{"userid":1329,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1329-elvesofzion","user":"ElvesofZion","date":". 11-01-2016, 03:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1329-elvesofzion","name":"ElvesofZion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1329_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-12T08:13:51-08:00","id":1002222,"title":"","content":"Hy, Storytellers<br /> <br /> In first place, I wanna say that I'm really gratefull, my friends, for you shared your opinions about the Social maneuvering system. I just read the posts and - since my oportunities to test the mechanics with my group has been thinning - learn from another ST means a lot to me. So, Thanks!<br /> <br /> About the situation that made me wrote this post... Well, the Social System was a nice improvement, I must say. Through it the Blood Doll (The Player Character) was able to convince her reticent vampiric lover to show her his haven, and stuff... but the goal she was really being planning (be embraced) ended up resolved by the very consequences of the story by itself: Another kindred fell for the girl ... and now she's the premium. =D","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11934-draugr","name":"Draugr","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11934_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}